<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The AI loop: an OUTER machine that drives an INNER agent session.

  The inner session is a pane sprag opened (`open_pane`) running an agent CLI.
  This machine never talks to that agent; it decides WHEN the driver should
  prompt it, when to stop, and when the session itself has to be replaced. The
  driver (sprag-plugin) owns every effect — assembling prompt text, reading the
  pane, editing files, closing and reopening the pane.

  Why an inner session is REPLACED rather than reconfigured: the things a loop
  wants to improve about itself — the agent's base context, which MCP servers
  are loaded, CLAUDE.md, the memory index — are all read when a session starts.
  A live session cannot be asked to re-read them. So `reflecting` settles what
  the next session should be working on and `restarting` closes the pane and
  opens a fresh one, which is briefed with it on the way up. That is the whole
  reason this machine has a restart state instead of a "reconfigure" event.

  ⚠⚠⚠ AND `reflecting` ASKS THE AGENT. The one thing a driver cannot decide is
  where somebody else's work should go next, so the reflection is a TURN: the
  agent that has been doing the work says what the next milestone is and what
  the next session should read, and those two answers are what cross the
  boundary. Without it a run is bounded by the checkpoint whoever started it
  wrote down — it can replace its session and greet the replacement with the
  same instruction for ever, which is a run bounded by one agent's context
  wearing a longer life.

  The terminal outcomes are the Driver's (`orchestration.scxml`) on purpose: a
  loop that ends reports the same shapes any plugin run reports, so one reader
  understands both.

  Where this machine DIFFERS from the Driver is `blocked`, and the difference is
  the whole reason a person can supervise a run. In the Driver a blocked peer
  ends the run, because nobody is watching. Here the inner session is a pane on
  a person's screen: they can read every turn as it happens and type into it
  directly. So an agent that stops to ASK sends this machine to `awaiting_human`
  — it stops prompting and waits. If the person answers the dialog, the agent
  goes back to work and the loop resumes where it was. `blocked` stays as the
  terminal for the case that actually ends a run: nobody came.

  ⚠⚠⚠ THIS DOCUMENT USED TO SAY *"the loop still never answers a dialog itself
  … answering is still not this machine's to do"*, and that sentence is now
  FALSE IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. It is kept here, struck through, because what
  replaced it is the shape of the whole feature rather than a detail:

  * A CALLER may declare consents (`AiLoopSpec::may_answer`), and the barrier
    takes an offered option on them. That is somebody saying *yes* in advance,
    and R382 measured what its absence cost: a loop whose agent asked one
    permission question stopped with ZERO turns judged, and every milestone
    that touches a file ends there.
  * The AUTHOR may write standing instructions (`screen_rules`), and `screening`
    carries them out by REFUSING the call and saying what to do instead.

  The sentence was right about the principle and wrong about the conclusion.
  Whose decision a tool permission is has not changed: it is the person's — and
  both of these are the person having made it IN ADVANCE, in writing, rather
  than the machine deciding. What the loop still never does is decide something
  nobody wrote down; a dialog neither a consent nor a rule reaches still stops
  the run and waits for somebody.

  datamodel="ecmascript" (not the `null` its two siblings use) because a loop's
  prompts and goals ARE its definition: the whole point is that a person edits
  them. Its siblings hold pure topology and keep their data in Rust beside the
  engine; here the data is the part being authored, so it lives in the document
  the author edits.

  ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT "ecmascript" IS EVALUATED BY, MEASURED. At the pinned SCE rev there
  is exactly one IScriptEngine — LuaEngine; sce-rust-runtime's own manifest
  calls QuickJS "future". SCE's build special-cases only the datamodel string
  "null" and routes every other value to whatever engine the consumer supplies,
  with no diagnostic. So every expression below is evaluated as LUA, and the
  generated init strings show the codegen rewriting some of the syntax on the
  way in (`[...]` -> `{...}`, `key:` -> `key =`) and not other parts of it
  (`'a' + b` is passed through, where Lua `+` is arithmetic).

  Reading that, this round predicted the composed prompts would fail to
  initialise. THEY DO NOT: `ai_loop.rs`'s gate drives a real LuaEngine and every
  shape here crosses whole — the bare literal, the `+`-composed prompt, the list
  of objects, the scalar. The prediction was wrong and the gate is what said so.

  What that measurement does NOT license is assuming ECMAScript semantics.
  Nothing here has been shown to work beyond the shapes the gate drives, so
  before writing `===`, `JSON.stringify`, `Array.prototype.map`, `undefined` or a
  template literal into this file, ADD IT TO THAT GATE FIRST. The engine will
  answer; the name at the top of this document will not.

  ⚠ AND `datamodel="lua"` IS NOT AN OPTION, measured at the pinned rev: SCE types
  the attribute and refuses `lua` as *undefined* (and `xpath` as *unimplemented*),
  so `ecmascript` is the only legal declaration for a script datamodel. The
  mismatch above is upstream's shape, not something this file can correct.

  ⚠⚠ NON-ASCII REACHES THIS DATAMODEL BY BOTH ROUTES INTO IT, and that is worth
  writing down because for one round it did not:

  * TEXT AUTHORED HERE, as a `<data expr="'…'">` literal — the three `screen_rules`
    replies below are Korean — always crossed whole.
  * TEXT ARRIVING AS EVENT DATA did not. `sce-rust-lua` rewrote a JSON payload into
    Lua source with `bytes[i] as char`, a Latin-1 decode of UTF-8, so a `brief` in
    a person's own language came back mangled with no error raised anywhere. The
    driver's read-back REFUSED it rather than prompting an agent with the result,
    which is how it was found at all; filed as PR-87 and FIXED upstream, where the
    rewrite was replaced by a real JSON decoder (restoring escapes and arrays with
    it — valid JSON carrying either had been demoting `_event.data` to a string).

  Both routes are measured by `a_non_ascii_string_reaches_the_datamodel_by_either
  _route`, which is what goes red if either regresses.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       version="1.0"
       name="ai_loop"
       datamodel="ecmascript"
       initial="idle">

  <!--
    Everything above the line is the template's editable surface. A person edits
    it here; a caller that did not write the file fills the same parts in through
    the `brief` event on `idle`. Nothing below the line changes per loop.

    ⚠⚠⚠ THE PARTS ARE DATA AND THE PROMPTS ARE COMPOSED FROM THEM IN `priming`,
    which is the one arrangement that lets both authors work. Composing in
    `<data>` — where this document did it until now — happens exactly once, at
    `initialize()`, so a caller filling a part in afterwards left the composed
    prompt stale and there was no way to reach it: MEASURED, the only prompt any
    caller could make this loop send was

        North star: (edit me) the outcome this loop exists to reach
        Milestone: (edit me) the next checkpoint on the way there
        Reference: (edit me) paths, URLs or repos to consult

    — three of the five clauses a live agent reads. Writing the composition in
    BOTH places would keep the file-editing author working and is what this
    document already warns against two comments down: two copies of one rule,
    and the failure of letting them drift is silent. `priming` is where it runs
    instead, once, because it is entered on the way to EVERY prompt this loop
    ever sends — from `idle` at the start, and from `restarting` after a
    reflection has rewritten the parts.
  -->
  <datamodel>
    <!-- Where this loop is ultimately going. Never rewritten by reflection. -->
    <data id="north_star" expr="'(edit me) the outcome this loop exists to reach'"/>
    <!-- The step being worked on now. Reflection may rewrite this. -->
    <data id="milestone" expr="'(edit me) the next checkpoint on the way there'"/>
    <!-- Prior art the agent should read before deciding anything. -->
    <data id="reference" expr="'(edit me) paths, URLs or repos to consult'"/>
    <!--
      The agent the driver launches in the inner pane. INTERACTIVE, never
      `-p`/print mode: sprag reads an agent's state from its TUI — the title's
      leading glyph separates working from idle, and a blocked agent is
      recognised by the shape of its dialog. Print mode renders none of that,
      so a `-p` inner session is one this loop cannot observe and a person
      cannot answer.
    -->
    <data id="model" expr="'claude'"/>

    <!--
      Termination: the loop converges when the agent says this.

      ⚠⚠⚠ DECLARED BEFORE THE PROMPTS, and that is load-bearing rather than
      tidy: `<data>` is evaluated in document order, and every working prompt
      below COMPOSES this string. Move it back down and the prompts initialise
      against a marker that does not exist yet.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE PROMPTS COMPOSE IT RATHER THAN REPEATING IT. R379 measured
      what the alternative costs. The marker existed here, `judging` waited for
      it, the driver looked for it on the screen — and NO PROMPT EVER ASKED THE
      AGENT FOR IT. Nothing in the loop was wrong except that the one word the
      whole termination rests on was never said to the only party who could say
      it back. A run against a real agent could not converge; it could only
      spend all `max_turns` and report `exhausted`, which is the word for *the
      budget ran out* and not for *nobody ever asked*. It went unnoticed for
      eleven rounds because the stand-in agent in the driver's gate was written
      to say the marker, which is a fixture manufacturing the answer it tests
      for.

      Composing means an author who edits the marker edits the instruction with
      it. Retyping the word into each prompt would be three places to keep in
      step, and the failure of doing that badly is silent.
    -->
    <data id="done_marker" expr="'MILESTONE REACHED'"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ AND WHAT THE AGENT SAYS WHEN THERE IS NOTHING LEFT TO DO AT ALL.

      `done_marker` ends a MILESTONE; this ends the RUN. Until both existed a
      reached milestone was the end of everything: `judging` sent a run that had
      finished one step straight to `closing`, so a loop could only ever be as
      long as its first checkpoint.

      MEASURED, on the owner's own run: an agent asked to repay debt paid ONE
      item, said `MILESTONE REACHED`, and the run converged after a single
      working turn — judged straight into `closing`, and converged. The
      request had been *"keep going until every debt is repaid"*, and no path
      through this document could express it. There was nothing wrong with the
      loop; the document simply had no edge from *this step is done* to *what is
      the next one*.

      ⚠⚠ TWO MARKERS AND NOT ONE, because they are two different claims a person
      would want reported differently: *"the thing you asked for is done"* and
      *"and there is no more of it"*. A run that converged on the first would end
      whenever its agent finished anything.

      ⚠ It is asked for in the REFLECTION and nowhere else. A working turn is
      about the milestone in front of it; whether the whole journey is over is a
      judgement about the journey, which is what a reflection is for.
    -->
    <data id="north_star_marker" expr="'NORTH STAR REACHED'"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE TWO LABELS A REFLECTION'S ANSWER IS READ BACK THROUGH, and they are
      authored for `done_marker`'s reason: they are said to somebody's agent, in
      somebody's language, and the driver must never be the thing that writes
      words into a prompt. Declared BEFORE the prompts because `priming` composes
      `reflect_prompt` out of them.

      ⚠⚠⚠ A LABEL AND NOT A WHOLE ROW, where `done_marker` is a whole row, and
      the difference is what each has to carry. *Done* is a yes or a no, so the
      marker IS the answer and `said_done` can demand it stand alone. A milestone
      is a SENTENCE the agent writes, so the label can only ever open the row it
      is on — which is a weaker rule, and the driver pays for that weakness in
      two places rather than pretending it does not exist (see
      `OuterLoop::proposed`): the row must OPEN with the label, and the text
      after it must not be something this loop itself said.

      ⚠ NEITHER APPEARS AT THE START OF A LINE IN THE PROMPT BELOW, deliberately.
      The instruction that asks for them names them mid-sentence, so the peer's
      echo of the prompt does not produce a row that opens with one. That is a
      mitigation and not a proof — a terminal wraps where it likes — which is
      why the driver's second rule exists at all.
    -->
    <data id="milestone_marker" expr="'NEXT MILESTONE:'"/>
    <data id="reference_marker" expr="'NEXT REFERENCE:'"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE FOUR COMPOSED PROMPTS ARE DECLARED EMPTY HERE AND WRITTEN IN
      `priming`. Their text is up there, in one place, where a person editing
      this template reads it; declaring them is what makes them assignable, and
      what lets a consumer ask whether this machine is one it can drive at all.

      A loop sitting in `idle` therefore holds three empty strings, and that is
      the honest answer rather than a gap: nothing has been primed, so nothing
      has been composed. What a caller can read before then is the PARTS, which
      are what it filled in.
    -->
    <data id="done_instruction" expr="''"/>
    <!-- Sent once, into a freshly opened session. -->
    <data id="start_prompt" expr="''"/>
    <!-- Sent on every turn after the first. -->
    <data id="turn_prompt" expr="''"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ SENT WHEN THE LOOP STOPS TO IMPROVE ITSELF, AND IT IS A TURN LIKE ANY
      OTHER — the agent is asked, and what it answers is what the NEXT session
      is briefed with.

      This is the whole difference between a run bounded by one agent's context
      and one that outlives it. Until this prompt existed a reflection could
      carry only what the AUTHOR had written down — the standing instructions a
      `screen_rule` had fired — so a loop replaced its session and greeted the
      replacement with the same milestone somebody wrote before any of the work
      had been done. A run meant to carry on past one checkpoint could only
      re-issue the first one for ever.

      ⚠⚠ WHERE THE WORK SHOULD GO NEXT IS A JUDGEMENT ABOUT THE WORK, so the
      only party that can make it is the agent that has been doing it. The
      driver reads the answer back and hands it over; it never writes one.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND IT DOES NOT SPEND `max_turns`. A reflection turn is not judged —
      `reflecting` does not pass through `judging` — so the budget still counts
      the turns the agent spent on the WORK. What bounds reflections is
      `reflect_every`, which is what makes their number a function of the
      budget rather than a second unbounded cost.
    -->
    <data id="reflect_prompt" expr="''"/>
    <!--
      Sent once before the loop reports converged. NOT composed — it names
      nothing else in this datamodel — so it stays a plain literal, and a
      reader can tell the two kinds apart by whether `priming` writes them.
    -->
    <data id="end_prompt"
          expr="'Summarise what changed, what was verified, and what is left open.'"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE SAME REQUEST MADE OF A RUN THAT IS ENDING WITHOUT GETTING
      THERE — `stopping`'s prompt, and the answer to *"an exhausted run has no
      account at all"*.

      Until this existed, `closing` was the ONLY state that asked its agent for
      anything, so the account belonged to the one ending that needed it least: a
      run that reached its north star has plainly succeeded, and the person who
      started it can see that from the word alone. **The ending a person most
      wants an account of is the one that stopped short**, and that ending
      answered with the single word `exhausted`.

      ⚠⚠ IT IS A DIFFERENT QUESTION AND NOT A COPY, which is the whole reason it
      is a second string rather than `end_prompt` reused. *"Summarise what
      changed, what was verified, and what is left open"* is asked of an agent
      that FINISHED; it presumes a body of completed work to summarise, and an
      agent that has run out of turns mid-edit would answer it by describing a
      thing that does not exist. What a person picking this run up needs instead
      is where it got to and what to do first — so this asks for that.

      ⚠ NOTHING OF THE BRIEF IS COMPOSED IN, for `end_prompt`'s reason: the agent
      being asked has been briefed with the north star and the milestone since
      `priming`, and naming them back at it buys nothing it does not already
      hold. What IS composed in is the one thing the agent cannot know and this
      document can — WHICH CEILING ended the run. See `stop_said` below.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND IT USED TO SAY THE WRONG ONE, TO THE ONE PARTY THAT COULD NOT CHECK.
      This was a single literal opening *"This run has spent its whole turn
      budget"*, and `stopping` is reached by FOUR ceilings: this document's own
      `max_turns`, and the RUN's three (`stop_short`, below). So for three of the
      four the sentence was FALSE — and it is not a journal line a reader can
      weigh, it is TYPED INTO A LIVE AGENT'S PANE in the one turn that asks that
      agent to account for itself. A run a wall clock stopped was told it had run
      out of turns, and whatever it wrote back about *what a run picking this up
      should do first* was reasoned from that. Register item 264, and item 261's
      class one state over: 261 misled a reader of a journal, this misled the
      WITNESS.
    -->
    <data id="stop_opening"
          expr="'This run is ending here, short of what it was asked for.'"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHICH CEILING ENDED IT, IN THIS DOCUMENT'S OWN WORDS — one clause per
      ceiling, keyed by the word the driver spells (`Ceiling::wire_str`).

      ⚠⚠⚠ THE KEYS ARE A CLOSED SET AND THIS MAP IS TOTAL OVER IT — AND THAT IS
      NOT A TIDINESS RULE. MEASURED, by deleting the `duration` clause and
      running the ceiling gate: a missing key does NOT make the `<assign>` in
      `stopping` fail and leave the shipped question standing. The concatenation
      SUCCEEDS and the agent is asked

        "This run is ending here, short of what it was asked for. nil Say where
         you got to: …"

      — the word `nil`, typed into a live agent's pane, in the turn that asks it
      to account for the run. So the gate that holds these keys equal to
      `Ceiling::ALL` is the only thing between a ceiling added in Rust months
      from now and that sentence. It reads THIS FILE, and it goes red the day the
      type grows an arm nobody wrote a clause for.

      ⚠ A DEFENSIVE FALLBACK WAS CONSIDERED AND REFUSED: `stop_said[stop_reason]`
      falling back to an empty clause would compose a true, ceiling-free question
      and nothing anywhere would say the map had a hole. The loud version is the
      one a gate can hold and a reader can see.

      ⚠ ONE PLACE, NOT ONE PER EDGE. `stopping` has two doors and four reasons —
      the outside one carries any of the run's three ceilings — so a clause
      written on the transitions would be the copy-drift this file warns against
      wherever it composes rather than repeats. A ceiling is one line to add here
      and one to delete.

      ⚠ THE PROSE IS THE AUTHOR'S BECAUSE AN AGENT READS IT. The driver knows
      which ceiling fell and says so in the run's JOURNAL, in its own words, to a
      person. What is said to somebody's agent, in somebody's language, is never
      the driver's to write — `done_instruction`'s rule, and the standing rules'.
    -->
    <data id="stop_said"
          expr="{
            turns:      'This run has spent every turn its document budgeted for it.',
            iterations: 'This run has taken every step its run was allowed.',
            cost:       'This run has spent everything its run was allowed to spend.',
            duration:   'This run has run out of the wall-clock time its run was given.'
          }"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠ WHAT IS ACTUALLY ASKED, kept apart from the opening so the ceiling
      clause has somewhere to go BETWEEN them. Every stand-in peer in this crate
      keys its answer on a verbatim slice of this, and one paints a second slice
      as its wrapped echo — reworded, a peer stops recognising the question, its
      turn never ends, and the gate reports a wall clock instead of a budget.
    -->
    <data id="stop_asking"
          expr="'Say where you got to: what you finished, what you left half-done, and what a run picking this up should do first.'"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠ SHIPPED WITHOUT A CEILING CLAUSE AND TRUE THAT WAY, which is what a
      caller previewing a loop should see: WHICH ceiling will end a run is not
      known until one does, and a preview that named one would be this defect one
      layer out. `stopping` composes the clause in when the answer exists.

      ⚠ COMPOSED FROM THE PARTS RATHER THAN FROM ITSELF, so entering `stopping`
      twice would compose the same sentence twice rather than accumulate — the
      state cannot be re-entered today (every one of its transitions targets a
      final), and this does not rest on that staying true.
    -->
    <data id="stop_prompt" expr="stop_opening + ' ' + stop_asking"/>

    <!--
      Standing instructions for dialogs the person has already decided about.
      When the inner agent raises a question no consent covers, `screening`
      matches it against these before anybody is woken; a match TURNS THE CALL
      DOWN and tells the agent what to do instead, and the run carries on.

      This is not the loop deciding. It is the loop CARRYING OUT a decision the
      person made in advance and wrote down — which is why the rules live in the
      authored half of this document and not in the driver. A rule with no match
      wakes the person, exactly as before.

      ⚠⚠⚠ EACH RULE IS `when` (WORDS THE DIALOG CARRIES) AND `text` (WHAT TO SAY
      ONCE THE CALL IS REFUSED), and both halves of that shape were decided by
      measurement rather than by argument.

      * `when` USED TO NAME A DIALOG *KIND* — 'design-decision', 'multiple-choice'
        — which needed somebody to classify other people's dialogs into a
        taxonomy, and `sprag-detect` classifies none. R383 asked a live agent
        instead: three tool families ask three DIFFERENT questions and one clause
        quoting the agent's own words covers all three. A taxonomy buys nothing
        quoting does not already have, and it would rot the first time an agent
        reworded anything. So a rule quotes, exactly as a consent's `asked` does,
        and they share one matcher.

      * `keys` IS GONE, AND ITS ABSENCE IS THE SAFETY PROPERTY. R384 pressed
        Escape, Tab and the offered `3` at a live claude's permission dialog:
        **Escape REFUSES THE TOOL CALL** (`User rejected write to PROBE.txt`, the
        file never created) in 25 ms, identically to `3. No` — while **Tab leaves
        the dialog up** and rewrites option 1 into *"Yes, and tell Claude what to
        do next"*, so the probe that typed into it **had its file written**.

        A rule that could name its own key could therefore name the key that
        APPROVES. The key is the product's now (`sprag_plugin::screen::REFUSES`),
        and a rule authors only which dialog and what to say — so a standing
        instruction cannot grant a permission, structurally rather than by hope.

      ⚠⚠ AND THAT IS WHY THERE IS NO `screen_permissions` FLAG ANY MORE. It read:
      *"approving a permission grants an agent the ability to act, and a standing
      yes is a different kind of promise than a standing think-about-it-again;
      nobody gets it by writing a rule that happened to match."* Every word of
      that is still true and none of it needs a flag: the act's first key is a
      REFUSAL, so no rule can reach the promise the flag was guarding. What the
      flag could never have done is tell a permission dialog from a design one —
      it needed the very taxonomy R383 measured away.

      ⚠ THE SHIPPED RULE IS A PLACEHOLDER, in this document's own `(edit me)`
      idiom, and deliberately claims nothing. A shipped needle would have to be
      invented: the dialogs a loop meets are MEASURED for tool permissions and
      unmeasured for design questions, and quoting words nobody has seen is the
      mistake this file made the last time. A loop that meets a dialog no rule
      claims says so in its own report (`no_rule`, naming the dialog), so an
      unedited placeholder is visible rather than silent.
    -->
    <data id="screen_rules"
          expr="[
            { when: '(edit me) words the dialog carries, quoted from the agent',
              text: '비용 무시하고 가장 장기적으로 올바른 방법으로 다시 생각하고 진행' }
          ]"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT MAKES A DIALOG ONE THIS LOOP TURNS DOWN, and what it says instead.

      A `screen_rule` above QUOTES a dialog. This JUDGES one — and the difference
      is not a preference, it is a measured limit. These two dialogs ask the
      identical question:

          Do you want to create PROBE.txt?    <- writes the word "ready"
          Do you want to create DESIGN.txt?   <- commits 256 lines that chose
                                                 JSON over SQLite

      No quote separates them, because what differs is what the action MEANS,
      and that is in the diff the dialog carries rather than in its wording.

      ⚠⚠ THIS IS NOT THE DIALOG-KIND TAXONOMY COMING BACK. R383 removed one
      because it asked an author to name a KIND from a list somebody else
      maintained, and `sprag-detect` classifies none. A criterion is the author's
      own sentence about their own run, decided per dialog against that dialog's
      actual contents rather than looked up in a table.

      ⚠ MEASURED, which is why it is spellable at all: a cheap judge (haiku, in
      print mode) separated four captured live dialogs 3 times
      out of 3, the identical-sentence pair included, at 4-6 s per call. The
      verdict is the FIRST WORD of the reply — the same model answers some
      dialogs in one word and others with a paragraph, so a reader taking the
      whole reply would call a stable verdict unstable.

      ⚠⚠⚠ A LIST, SO A DECISION IS ONE ELEMENT TO ADD AND ONE TO DELETE. Each
      carries three things and nothing else:

        name   what to call it. The driver publishes this as
               `_event.data.rule`, so a document that wants DIFFERENT handling
               per decision routes on it and one that does not can ignore it.
        judge  the sentence a second agent answers about ONE dialog.
        text   what the agent is told once that dialog has been refused.

      Shaped after `screen_rules` above deliberately: an author who has written
      one has written the other, and the only difference is whether the claim is
      quoted or judged. To add a decision, add an element:

        { name: 'security',
          judge: 'going ahead would weaken an authentication or authorisation
                  boundary',
          text:  'Stop and state the boundary this crosses before continuing.' }

      ⚠⚠ THE EMPTY LIST IS THE DEFAULT AND DECLINES THIS ENTIRELY: no judge is
      asked, no model call is paid for, and every blocked turn goes to
      `screening` exactly as it did before this existed. A run that did not ask
      for a second agent must not acquire one.

      ⚠ AND A LIST HERE IS ONLY HALF. The other half is WHO answers — the
      caller's `judge` argument — which lives nowhere in this document. Rules
      with nobody to ask change nothing, which is a run's default state.
    -->
    <data id="judged_rules" expr="[]"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ HOW A STANDING INSTRUCTION IS INTRODUCED once it has been carried out,
      and this prose is AUTHORED for the same reason the rules' own `text` is: it
      is said to somebody's agent, in somebody's language, and the driver must
      never be the thing that writes words into a prompt.

      A rule fires ONCE — `screening` refuses the call, types the instruction and
      the turn carries on — and until this existed that was the whole of its
      life. R384's live agent measured what that costs, in its own words:

          "the loop is re-issuing the original milestone, but your last direct
           instruction was to not create that file … 루프가 매 턴 같은 요청을
           반복하고 저는 매 턴 같은 이유로 거절하게 되므로, 진전이 없습니다."

      Measured offline as two numbers on a six-turn run: the instruction reached
      the pane ONCE and the milestone it overrides was asked for SIX times. So an
      instruction that has to STICK has to reach every later prompt, which means
      it has to be one of the PARTS the prompts are composed from — and the
      composition happens in `priming`, so adopting one is a session
      replacement. That is what `reflecting` and `restarting` are for.
    -->
    <data id="standing_label"
          expr="'A standing instruction you have already been given, which overrides the milestone wherever the two conflict: '"/>

    <!--
      Budgets. `max_turns` bounds the run; `reflect_every` sets how often
      the loop stops to improve itself. `judged_rules` is beside them because a
      judged dialog costs a model call too — see its own comment.

      ⚠⚠⚠ BOTH ARE COUNTS OF TURNS, AND A TURN IS NOT A UNIT OF ANYTHING THE
      BILL IS DENOMINATED IN. Measured over 39 local sessions: what one billed
      request adds to the agent's context is 861 tokens at the median, 2,989 at
      p90, 10,402 at p99 and 633,749 at the maximum. A `date` invocation and a
      3,000-line file read are both one turn.

      * `max_turns` IS NOT A COST CEILING. It is honestly named — it bounds
        turns — and `Ceiling::Turns` says so. But two runs both ending
        `exhausted, 40 of 40` are not comparable on spend, because cost tracks
        accumulated context and context per turn spans three orders of
        magnitude. A caller who wants a budget wants a different ceiling, and
        this loop does not offer one.

      * `reflect_every` IS ALSO A COST KNOB, WHICH IS NOT WHY IT IS HERE.
        `reflecting` exits through `restarting`, which throws the accumulated
        context away and pays a cold start to rebuild the fixed prefix. Whether
        that trade wins depends on how much there was to throw away:
        `claudedocs/INSIGHT-RESTART-COST-CURVE.md` measured a restart paying for
        itself only above roughly 15,000-21,000 tokens of discardable context,
        and measured this default losing by 11.4% on its workload.

        ⚠ THAT RESULT DOES NOT SETTLE THE DEFAULT, and the reason is worth
        writing down rather than rounding off. The experiment's task accumulated
        755 tokens per turn; real sessions in this repo accumulate 1,745. At the
        real rate eight turns discard about 14,000 — ON the threshold, not far
        under it. So the sign of the trade at `reflect_every = 8` is UNMEASURED
        for real work, and 16 or 24 would clear the threshold outright.

      ⚠⚠ SO DO NOT TUNE EITHER NUMBER ON COST ALONE. What `reflect_every` is
      actually for is how often the loop gets to improve its own setup, and
      whether eight turns is the right cadence FOR THAT has never been measured
      at all. Changing it to save money would trade a measured axis against an
      unmeasured one.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND IF A RESTART POLICY IS EVER WRITTEN, IT MUST NOT READ THESE
      COUNTERS. Predicting context from turn count is off by 19% at the median,
      63% at p90 and 30x at worst — against a 15-21k threshold, an eight-turn
      estimate carries a band that straddles the entire decision. The exact
      quantity is reported on every billed request as `cache_read_input_tokens`,
      and an interactive inner session writes it to
      `~/.claude/projects/<cwd-slug>/<session>.jsonl` even though it publishes
      no structured JSON result envelope for `reply.rs` to read (that is print
      mode's `output-format json`, which this loop forbids). Nothing needs to be
      instrumented; the number is already being written down.
    -->
    <data id="max_turns" expr="40"/>
    <data id="reflect_every" expr="8"/>

    <!-- ── below the line: loop bookkeeping, not authored ── -->
    <data id="turns" expr="0"/>
    <data id="turns_since_reflect" expr="0"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT THE INNER SESSION HAS BEEN CHARGED TO READ, as of its last billed
      request. Written by the driver on every `turn.done` and by nothing else.

      This is the number the two budgets above are NOT. Measured across forty
      local agent sessions: cache read is 99.0% of tokens and 78.1% of cost,
      while the part a prompt's size resembles is 10.3% of cost — and it is the
      part that FALLS as a session grows. A turn is not a unit of it either: one
      request adds 861 tokens at the median and 633,749 at the maximum.

      ⚠⚠ ZERO MEANS "DO NOT DECIDE ON THIS", not "nothing has accumulated". A run
      whose session cannot be named — an agent sprag did not name, a host that
      cannot read a process, an agent that has not written yet — reads 0, and the
      two are indistinguishable here on purpose: the alternative is refusing to
      drive a loop over a number that is only ever an optimisation.

      ⚠ NOTHING GUARDS ON IT YET, and that is deliberate rather than unfinished.
      What it would be for is deciding whether `restarting` is worth its cold
      start, and the threshold that decides is measured in
      `claudedocs/INSIGHT-RESTART-COST-CURVE.md` at 15,000-21,000 tokens of
      discardable context — a figure whose own sign for real workloads is still
      unmeasured. A guard written before that settles would be a policy nobody
      can defend; the number arriving is what makes settling it possible.
    -->
    <data id="context" expr="0"/>
    <!-- ⚠⚠ How many calls a standing instruction TURNED DOWN. Counted so an
         auto-screened run does not read like a hand-answered one.

         ⚠ Incremented on `screen.matched` and NOT on entry to `screening`,
         which is where it used to sit and where it counted the wrong thing:
         entering that state means a dialog was LOOKED at, and this variable's
         name promises the ones a rule actually acted on. A loop whose rules
         claim nothing would have counted every question it declined to
         answer. -->
    <data id="screened" expr="0"/>
    <!-- ⚠⚠ How many dialogs a JUDGEMENT turned down, kept apart from `screened`
         for the reason that variable is kept apart from a hand-answered run: two
         different authorities acted, and a reader of a finished run must be able
         to tell which. A quote the author wrote is auditable by re-reading the
         document; a judgement is not, so its count is the only trace it leaves. -->
    <data id="redirected" expr="0"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE STANDING INSTRUCTIONS THIS RUN HAS CARRIED OUT, one labelled line
      each, accumulated on `screen.matched` and composed into both working
      prompts by `priming`.

      It is BOOKKEEPING and not authored: the words are the author's (the rule's
      own `text`, behind the author's own `standing_label`), and what this
      variable holds is which of them have actually happened. A person editing
      this file has nothing to say here.

      ⚠ The label is repeated per line rather than written once above the list,
      and that is deliberate: making it a heading would need this composition to
      know whether the list is EMPTY, and a conditional in executable content is
      a construct this document has never been measured with. A self-describing
      line costs a few words and needs no `<if>`.

      ⚠⚠ IT HAS NO CEILING, and that is stated rather than capped. Each DISTINCT
      instruction adds one line to every prompt the loop sends for the rest of
      the run (repeats are dropped — the driver normalises at every reflection),
      so a long run whose agent asks about many different things pays for all of
      them on every turn. What the right bound would be is a question about
      somebody else's work — the oldest instruction is not obviously the least
      important — so nothing here guesses at one.
    -->
    <data id="standing" expr="''"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT THE REVIEW OF THE RUN'S OWN CLOSED SESSIONS HANDED BACK — one
      line, or empty.

      It is `standing`'s SIBLING and not `standing` itself, because the two are
      different kinds of instruction and merging them would make one of them a
      lie. A standing instruction is a thing a person or a judge decided, and
      `standing_label` says it *overrides the milestone wherever the two
      conflict*. This is an observation about how the last few sessions SPENT
      THEMSELVES — «one log was tailed eighty-four times» — which overrides
      nothing and is nobody's ruling.

      ⚠⚠⚠ IT GOES INTO `start_prompt` ONLY, and that is the point of WHEN the
      review runs. The review happens between the reflection and the restart, so
      the party it has something to tell is the session that does not exist yet —
      one that remembers none of the habit and is about to repeat it. In
      `turn_prompt` the same line would be re-sent on every turn of a session
      that has already read it: a cost `standing` pays deliberately and this has
      no reason to.

      ⚠ It holds its own line terminator and is EMPTY until a review carries
      something, so a loop whose reviews find nothing composes exactly the text
      it always did — `standing`'s arrangement, for `standing`'s reason: no
      conditional in executable content.

      ⚠⚠ IT IS OVERWRITTEN, NEVER APPENDED. Each review speaks about the sessions
      it has just read, so an older reading is not evidence about the current
      one — and a list that only grew would carry a broken habit into every
      prompt of a run that had already stopped doing it.
    -->
    <data id="carried" expr="''"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠ How many screened calls the loop has already ADOPTED — the value
      `screened` had at the last reflection.

      `judging` compares the two, so a standing instruction that fired is
      reflected on at the very next judgement rather than waiting out
      `reflect_every`. Without it the loop asks for the original milestone again
      on the turn straight after being told not to, which is the defect above.
    -->
    <data id="screened_carried" expr="0"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHY THIS REFLECTION WAS ASKED FOR, written by whichever `judge`
      transition reached `reflecting`.

      Three roads lead there and they are NOT the same question:

        milestone     the agent said the checkpoint is reached, so the only
                      thing left to settle is what the NEXT one is
        instruction   a standing instruction fired that the prompts do not
                      carry yet
        budget        `reflect_every` came round

      Registered as owed before it existed (*"a run cannot say WHY its session
      was replaced … a person whose agent's session was just replaced has no way
      to tell because you told it something from because eight turns went by"*),
      and what forced it is that the three need DIFFERENT endings. A reflection
      asked because the milestone is done, which then names no next milestone,
      must END THE RUN: sending it back to `working` would ask an agent to reach
      a checkpoint it has just reached, for ever. The other two go back to work,
      because the work is not finished.

      ⚠ It is read by the DRIVER, in Rust, and never by a `cond` here: `==` over
      this datamodel is unverified (see `judged_rules`), and a guard that
      compared strings would be the first place to find that out.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND IT REACHES A PERSON NOW, WHICH IS THE HALF THAT WAS MISSING. The
      paragraph above says the three need different ENDINGS and that was all this
      variable ever bought: the driver read it to decide one of them, and a reader
      of the run got the same six words whichever had fired. It is now also the
      journal's word for the edge into `reflecting` — see the driver's
      `ReflectReason`, whose arms are these three and no others, held against this
      list by a gate that reads this file.
    -->
    <data id="reflect_reason" expr="''"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHICH CEILING ENDED THE RUN, written by whichever `judge` transition
      reached `stopping` — `reflect_reason`'s twin, one state over, and for the
      same finding.

      Two transitions reach `stopping` and FOUR facts arrive on them: this
      document's own `turns >= max_turns`, and the run's `iterations`, `cost` and
      `duration`, which all come in through the one outside door
      (`_event.data.stop_short`, whose value IS the driver's word for the ceiling
      that bound). Before this existed neither reader could tell them apart:

        the AGENT   was told, in `stop_prompt`, that it had run out of turns —
                    false for three of the four, and typed into its pane
                    (register item 264)
        a READER    saw one `judge` arrow from `judging` to `stopping` and
                    nothing else, whichever ceiling had fallen. The only
                    thing separating the two doors in a run's journal was
                    whether a `note_to_itself` line PRECEDED the edge — telling
                    them apart BY THE ABSENCE OF A KEY, which is the reading this
                    workspace has burned wire numbers over (register item 265)

      ⚠ It holds the DRIVER's word (`Ceiling::wire_str`) and not a word of this
      document's own, which is the one difference from `reflect_reason`: the fact
      is the run's, three quarters of the time. `turns` is spelled here because
      that ceiling is this document's and no driver can see it.

      ⚠ Read by the driver, in Rust, and never by a `cond` here — `==` over this
      datamodel is unverified (see `judged_rules`). `stop_said` above is indexed
      by it, which is a lookup rather than a comparison.
    -->
    <data id="stop_reason" expr="''"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHICH OF THE TWO ENDINGS CLOSED THE RUN — `reflect_reason`'s and
      `stop_reason`'s third, one state further on, and the same finding again.

      ONE transition reaches `closing` and TWO different runs take it:

        declared       the agent said `north_star_marker` — the whole job is
                       finished, and it says so
        no_successor   the milestone was reached and this reflection named no
                       next checkpoint, so there was nothing left to ask THIS
                       agent for. **Nobody said the work was done.**

      Both end `converged`, so before this existed a run that reported its own
      completion and a run that merely ran out of things to propose were the
      same three words in a journal — and they want opposite things from a
      reader. The first is an account to weigh against what was asked; the
      second is a checkpoint to look at and a north star to decide about.

      ⚠⚠⚠ THE WORD IS THE DRIVER'S, LIKE `stop_reason`'s AND UNLIKE
      `reflect_reason`'s, and here it is the driver's for BOTH of them. Neither
      fact is visible from this document: one is a marker on somebody's pane,
      the other is that marker's absence together with a reflection reason. So
      the value arrives as `_event.data.done_reason`, whose vocabulary is the
      driver's `DoneReason::word`, and this file spells no ending of its own.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE TRANSITION IS GUARDED ON IT, which is the difference between
      this and its two elders. There the causes are EDGES, so a cause that
      forgot to say why is a missing `<assign>` and a gate over this file finds
      it. Here they are `return`s in Rust, and no reader of this document can
      count them — so the guard is what holds the direction instead.

      ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT THE GUARD ACTUALLY BUYS, MEASURED BOTH WAYS by raising this event
      with no word (the first draft of this comment claimed something else and
      the mutation said otherwise):

        guarded     the edge does not fire. The walk writes a SELF-ARROW —
                    `reflecting` to `reflecting` on `reflect.done` — which no
                    correct run ever produces, and the pass falls through to
                    whichever ending the next look decides, so the run ends
                    saying the wrong one. Wrong, and visibly wrong.
        unguarded   the edge fires, `done_reason` is assigned `nil`, and the
                    walk writes the ordinary `reflecting` to `closing` line
                    with no clause on it: the shipped defect exactly, and
                    indistinguishable from a correct run.

      So the guard does NOT stop the run, which is what the first draft said it
      would. What it does is make a wordless raise unable to REACH this state,
      and leave a shape in the journal that cannot otherwise happen. That is the
      reading register items 264, 265 and 269 were all about, kept on the side
      where an absence is loud instead of silent.

      ⚠ Truthiness over Lua, exactly as `_event.data.stop_short` is: the only
      false values are `nil` and `false`, so an ending whose word was EMPTY
      would pass the guard and then read back as nothing. No `DoneReason` can
      spell itself `''` and a gate holds that rather than this comment.

      ⚠ Read by the driver, in Rust, and never by a `cond` here — `==` over this
      datamodel is unverified (see `judged_rules`).
    -->
    <data id="done_reason" expr="''"/>
  </datamodel>

  <state id="idle">
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE TEMPLATE, FILLED IN BY SOMEBODY WHO DID NOT EDIT THIS FILE.

      A person authoring a loop edits the `<data>` above. A GUI — or any caller
      that starts a run — has no file to edit, and until this transition existed
      it had no other way either: the composed prompts were built at
      `initialize()`, so writing a part afterwards left them stale, and the only
      prompt a caller could actually send was the `(edit me)` template.

      TARGETLESS ON PURPOSE. W3C SCXML 3.13: a transition with no `target`
      executes its content and changes no state, so a brief does not exit and
      re-enter `idle` — there is nothing to re-run here, because the composition
      lives in `priming` and every path to a prompt goes through it.

      ⚠ ONLY BEFORE THE RUN STARTS, and that is the whole reason it sits on
      `idle` rather than on the machine. A loop already driving an agent adopts
      new parts through `reflecting` — which writes them, restarts the session,
      and passes back through `priming` — because changing what a run is for
      underneath a working agent is a session replacement, not an assignment.
    -->
    <transition event="brief">
      <assign location="north_star" expr="_event.data.north_star"/>
      <assign location="milestone" expr="_event.data.milestone"/>
      <assign location="reference" expr="_event.data.reference"/>
      <assign location="max_turns" expr="_event.data.max_turns"/>
      <assign location="reflect_every" expr="_event.data.reflect_every"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠ THE STANDING INSTRUCTIONS TOO, and this assignment is UNCONDITIONAL, which is why the
        driver always sends them: a caller that says nothing about screening has the document's own
        rules read out and echoed back here. Omitting the key would assign nil and delete an
        author's rules for every caller that did not happen to care about them.
      -->
      <assign location="screen_rules" expr="_event.data.screen_rules"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="start" target="priming"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠ A LOOP CAN BE UNSTARTABLE BEFORE IT EVER PROMPTS, and until this edge
      existed `idle` was the one non-final state with no way out but forward.
      What made it concrete: a brief whose text the script engine could not
      carry is already assigned by the time anybody can tell, so the run must
      not be startable — and with no `fail` here the driver's refusal could be
      walked straight past by pumping.
    -->
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    A session exists and has not been prompted yet. Entered at the very
    beginning and again after every restart, which is what makes a restarted
    loop resume with the CURRENT prompts rather than the ones it started with.
  -->
  <state id="priming">
    <onentry>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠ THE COMPOSITION, WRITTEN ONCE AND RUN ON EVERY PATH TO A PROMPT.

        `priming` is entered from `idle` at the start and from `restarting`
        after every session replacement, and those are the only two ways this
        loop ever comes to send a prompt. So composing here is composing exactly
        as often as the parts can have changed — a brief before the run, a
        reflection during it — with one copy of the text.

        ⚠ ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING, as it was in `<data>` before: `done_instruction`
        is composed FIRST because the two working prompts end with it. Executable
        content runs in document order, so moving it below them would append the
        PREVIOUS entry's instruction — silently, and correctly for every entry
        but the first.

        ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE PROMPTS COMPOSE `done_marker` RATHER THAN REPEATING IT. R379
        measured what the alternative costs. The marker existed, `judging` waited
        for it, the driver looked for it on the screen — and NO PROMPT EVER ASKED
        THE AGENT FOR IT. Nothing in the loop was wrong except that the one word
        the whole termination rests on was never said to the only party who could
        say it back. A run against a real agent could not converge; it could only
        spend all `max_turns` and report `exhausted`, which is the word for *the
        budget ran out* and not for *nobody ever asked*. It went unnoticed for
        eleven rounds because the stand-in agent in the driver's gate was written
        to say the marker, which is a fixture manufacturing the answer it tests
        for.

        Composing means an author who edits the marker edits the instruction with
        it. Retyping the word into each prompt would be three places to keep in
        step, and the failure of doing that badly is silent.

        How the agent is told to end a turn it believes finished the milestone is
        prose a person may want in their own language, which is why it is here
        and not in the driver — the driver must not be the thing that appends
        instructions to an authored prompt (that is a second author).
      -->
      <assign location="done_instruction"
              expr="'When the milestone is fully reached AND verified, make the last line of your ' +
                    'reply exactly: ' + done_marker"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠ `standing` IS COMPOSED INTO BOTH WORKING PROMPTS, and it is what makes
        a standing instruction outlive the turn it was given in. It holds its own
        line terminators and is EMPTY until a rule has fired, so a loop that has
        never been screened composes exactly the text it always did — which is
        why no conditional is needed here.

        ⚠ It goes into `start_prompt` too, and that is the half that matters most:
        `start_prompt` is what a FRESH session is greeted with after a restart,
        and a fresh agent remembers nothing of having been redirected. A loop
        that carried its instructions only in `turn_prompt` would hand its
        replacement a clean slate and the original milestone.
      -->
      <assign location="start_prompt"
              expr="'North star: ' + north_star + '\n' +
                    'Milestone: ' + milestone + '\n' +
                    'What to carry: ' + reference + '\n' +
                    standing +
                    carried +
                    'Report what you did and what is left.' + '\n' +
                    done_instruction"/>
      <assign location="turn_prompt"
              expr="'Continue toward: ' + milestone + '\n' +
                    standing +
                    'Do the next smallest thing that is verifiable, then report.' + '\n' +
                    done_instruction"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠ THE REFLECTION'S OWN PROMPT, composed here for the reason the other
        three are: it names `north_star`, `milestone` and `standing`, and a
        reflection that asked about a milestone the run had already moved past
        would collect an answer about the wrong work.

        ⚠⚠⚠ THE LABELS ARE NAMED MID-SENTENCE AND NEVER OPEN A LINE. The peer
        paints what it is given, so an instruction that began a line with
        `NEXT MILESTONE:` would put a row on the screen that the driver's own
        reader cannot tell from an answer. Written this way the echo carries the
        label with words in front of it, which is the shape the reader rejects.
        ⚠ It is a mitigation, not a proof — a terminal wraps where it likes —
        and the driver's second rule (*discount what this loop itself said*) is
        what actually decides. See `OuterLoop::proposed`.

        ⚠⚠ NO TOOL IS ASKED FOR, deliberately: a reflection is a question about
        work already done, and an agent that reaches for a tool here raises a
        dialog in a state that cannot screen one (see `reflecting`'s own
        comment). Asking for an answer from what it already knows is the cheapest
        way to keep the turn inside the state that owns it.

        ⚠⚠⚠ THE SECOND QUESTION ASKS FOR KNOWLEDGE, NOT FOR AN ADDRESS, AND A
        MEASUREMENT IS WHY. It used to ask *"what the next session should read
        first"*, and what came back was an address — one live reflection answered
        `debt-open.md의 208·212·213과 섹션 P 전체`. A pointer costs its reader the
        whole read. Measured across that replacement:

          the session that was replaced   reached its first change at 161,507
          the fresh one it briefed        reached its first change at 285,599

        The restart discarded 348,000 tokens of context and the replacement had
        bought 266,000 of it back within eighty requests. **A handover that names
        where to look hands over nothing; it hands over an errand.**

        So the question changed and the slot's own clause with it (`What to
        carry:`, above): a caller's first briefing may still be prior art, and
        everything a reflection puts there afterwards is what the last session had
        to work out.

        ⚠⚠ WHAT JUDGES IT IS NOT A UNIT TEST. This is authored prose, and prose
        cannot be gated by asserting the words back — that is a test agreeing with
        the renderer. What settles it is `Warmup` (`spend.rs`) on the NEXT
        replacement: **285,599 is the number to beat**, on comparable work. If it
        does not fall, the fault was not the wording and a distiller is owed.
      -->
      <assign location="reflect_prompt"
              expr="'North star: ' + north_star + '\n' +
                    'You have been working toward: ' + milestone + '\n' +
                    standing +
                    'Stop and decide what comes next, from what you have just done and what you now know. Answer from what you already have; do not use a tool.' + '\n' +
                    'Reply with exactly two lines and nothing else. Open the first with the label ' + milestone_marker +
                    ' and then the next checkpoint in one line. Open the second with the label ' + reference_marker +
                    ' and then, in one line, what the next session must KNOW that you had to work out — not what it should read.' + '\n' +
                    'If the north star itself is fully reached and nothing is left to do, ignore both labels and make the last line of your reply exactly: ' + north_star_marker"/>
      <send event="prompt.start"/>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="prompt.sent" target="working"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
  </state>

  <!-- The inner agent is running. The driver watches its pane. -->
  <state id="working">
    <transition event="turn.done" target="judging">
      <assign location="turns" expr="turns + 1"/>
      <assign location="turns_since_reflect" expr="turns_since_reflect + 1"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE AGENT ASKED, AND THE FIRST QUESTION IS WHAT KIND OF QUESTION IT IS.

      A dialog that would commit a DESIGN DECISION is turned down and redirected;
      anything else goes to `screening`, where the author's quoted rules and then
      the person decide as they always have.

      ⚠⚠⚠ WHY THIS IS A `cond` AND NOT A FLAG THE DRIVER READS. The routing IS
      the decision, so it belongs where a person editing this file can see it —
      two lines, in order, saying what happens to each kind. A driver that
      branched internally on the same fact would make this document say
      *"a blocked turn goes to screening"* while the product did something else.

      ⚠⚠ WHAT THE GUARD CANNOT DO, and why the driver still measures. Deciding
      whether a dialog commits a design decision needs a second agent reading the
      dialog's own contents — MEASURED, because the two dialogs it must separate
      say the identical sentence:

          Do you want to create PROBE.txt?    <- writes the word "ready"
          Do you want to create DESIGN.txt?   <- commits 256 lines that chose
                                                 JSON over SQLite

      No expression over this datamodel can tell those apart. And a guard must
      not try: SCXML does not promise how often it evaluates one, and the
      measured judgement takes 4-6 seconds. So the driver judges ONCE, when it
      raises the event, and publishes the verdict as `_event.data.design` — the
      same arrangement `judge`'s own `_event.data.done` has always used. The
      driver measures; the document decides.

      ⚠ A run with no judge configured, a judge that errs, and a judge that
      answers something which is not a verdict all arrive here as FALSE. Silence
      is not a yes: the alternative would refuse an agent's tool call on nobody's
      decision, which is the act `screen.rs` removed the `keys` field to prevent.
    -->
    <transition event="turn.blocked" cond="_event.data.judged" target="redirecting"/>
    <transition event="turn.blocked" target="screening"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠ ROUTING A SINGLE DECISION SOMEWHERE OF ITS OWN, if a document ever wants
      to. The driver publishes the matched rule's `name` as `_event.data.rule`
      beside the boolean, so a fork per decision is one more line above this one:

          <transition event="turn.blocked"
                      cond="_event.data.rule == 'security'"
                      target="halting"/>

      ⚠⚠⚠ NOT SHIPPED, AND NOT BECAUSE NOBODY WANTED IT. `==` over this datamodel
      is UNVERIFIED — the header's rule is that a shape not driven by a gate is
      not known to work here, and `===` is already recorded as one nobody has
      run. Add it to `ai_loop.rs`'s datamodel gate before writing it into a
      document somebody depends on.

      ⚠⚠⚠ THE BOOLEAN IS PUBLISHED SEPARATELY, AND THE REASON THIS USED TO GIVE
      WAS FALSE. It read: *"it is Lua, where the only false values are `nil` and
      `false`; an empty string is TRUE, so a guard reading the rule's name would
      fire on every blocked turn of a run that matched nothing."* That is Lua's
      rule and NOT this engine's — measured, and the engine's own source says so
      in one line:

          _scxml_truthy(val): nil/false -> false, 0/0.0/"" -> false, else true

      Every `cond` in every document here is wrapped in that call by the code
      generator, so an empty string is FALSE and the failure that paragraph
      predicted cannot happen.

      ⚠⚠ THE SHAPE STAYS, WITH ITS REAL REASON. A name and a verdict are two
      facts — *nothing matched* and *nobody judged* are different answers, and a
      document that wants to route on WHICH rule fired needs the name whatever
      the truthiness rules are. What changed is that this is no longer defended
      by a fact about the engine that was never driven by a gate.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND THAT IS THE LESSON, not the correction: the paragraph above this
      one says *"a shape not driven by a gate is not known to work here — add it
      to `ai_loop.rs`'s datamodel gate before writing it into a document"*, and
      the sentence four lines below it made an engine claim with no gate at all.
      `a_guard_reads_ecmascript_truth_and_not_lua_truth` is now that gate.
    -->

    <!--
      The person reached into the inner pane and interrupted the agent
      themselves (Escape, Ctrl-C, or just typing). They are plainly here and
      doing something, so the loop stops driving and waits — screening would be
      answering a question the person is already handling.
    -->
    <transition event="turn.interrupted" target="awaiting_human"/>
    <!-- A watching person can halt the loop between turns. -->
    <transition event="hold" target="awaiting_human"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    A dialog is up. Does a standing instruction cover it?

    A state rather than something the driver swallows, because a rule that fires
    is a thing that happened to the run: it consumed a turn, it steered the
    agent, and whoever reads the trace afterwards needs to see it. A screening
    step hidden inside the driver would make an auto-answered run and a
    hand-answered one look identical.
  -->
  <state id="screening">
    <onentry>
      <send event="screen.begin"/>
    </onentry>
    <!--
      A rule claimed it. The driver refused the call and told the agent what to
      do instead, and the agent is working on that — which is why this edge
      sends NO prompt (see `Owed::on`): a prompt here would type over a peer
      that has just been spoken to.
    -->
    <transition event="screen.matched" target="working">
      <assign location="screened" expr="screened + 1"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE INSTRUCTION IS REMEMBERED, not only said. The driver carries
        the rule's own `text` on this event; keeping it is what lets `priming`
        compose it into every later prompt once `reflecting` has adopted it. See
        `standing`.
      -->
      <assign location="standing"
              expr="standing + standing_label + _event.data.text + '\n'"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      NO STANDING INSTRUCTION GOT THE RUN PAST THIS ONE. The person decides.

      ⚠⚠ SEVERAL DIFFERENT THINGS reach this edge and the driver's own report is
      what tells them apart, because a state cannot carry a reason: no rule
      claimed the dialog (`no_rule`); one did and the refusing key did not take
      it off the screen (`not_dismissed`); one did, the key went in, and the RUN
      ENDED inside the wait, so nobody watched (`unwitnessed`). NOTHING WAS
      TYPED in any of them, and that is what this edge is for — a dialog still
      up reads an Enter as an answer to itself, which is measured, so the run
      stops rather than saying its piece.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE SENTENCE ABOVE USED TO SAY *TWO*, which stopped being true the
      round `Refused::StillUp` grew its second refusal and did not move with it.
      What this edge MEANS is *the question is still up and this run has nothing
      left to do about it*; WHICH refusals satisfy that is `Unanswered`'s
      business and `Refusal::ALL` is the only list of them. A count written here
      is a count nothing keeps in step.

      ⚠⚠ AND *THE DRIVER'S OWN REPORT* IS THE JOURNAL LINE, which is where that
      first sentence only became true. The step that takes this edge answers
      `continue` — `awaiting_human` is not final — so nothing structural on it
      carried the reason, and a run cancelled at the refusing key never reached a
      step that did. `AiLoop::walked` now writes the refusal the pass ARRIVED at
      onto the line itself.
    -->
    <transition event="screen.none" target="awaiting_human"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE DIALOG WENT AWAY BEFORE THE RULE COULD ACT, so nothing was pressed and the turn
      simply carries on.

      The question reaches `screening` from the reading the BARRIER took on the pump before, and in
      the gap a person can answer it or the agent can withdraw it. Without this edge the driver's
      only choices were to press into whatever is there now — Escape into a composer discards what
      a person was in the middle of typing — or to end the run over a dialog that resolved itself.
      Neither is what happened, and a machine that cannot say *nothing happened* forces its driver
      to pick a lie.

      ⚠ It carries no prompt and does NOT count as screened, for the same reason: the turn was
      never interrupted, the peer is still working on the prompt it already has, and this loop's
      `Completion` is still armed from it.
    -->
    <transition event="screen.moot" target="working"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    The run is paused and a person is expected. Two ways in: the agent raised a
    dialog it cannot pass without an answer, or somebody watching asked the loop
    to stop and let them look.

    Nothing is sent from here. The pane stays exactly as the person found it,
    which is what makes it readable — a loop that kept prompting while somebody
    was reading would move the screen out from under them.
  -->
  <state id="awaiting_human">
    <onentry>
      <!--
        The outer loop's own channel to the person. The driver raises a pane
        attention notification, which sprag surfaces in `list_panes` — so a
        waiting run is visible without anybody watching the screen for it.
      -->
      <send event="notify.human"/>
    </onentry>
    <!-- The person answered (or typed a turn themselves) and it completed. -->
    <transition event="turn.done" target="judging">
      <assign location="turns" expr="turns + 1"/>
      <assign location="turns_since_reflect" expr="turns_since_reflect + 1"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      The person interrupted again while the loop was already waiting — they are
      working in the pane by hand. Stay put; do not start prompting underneath
      somebody who is typing.
    -->
    <transition event="turn.interrupted" target="awaiting_human"/>
    <!-- The person looked and waved it on without touching anything. -->
    <transition event="resume" target="working">
      <send event="prompt.turn"/>
    </transition>
    <!-- Nobody came within the driver's patience. This is the run-ending one. -->
    <transition event="unattended" target="blocked"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    One turn has landed. Decide in priority order: goal met, budget spent,
    time to improve ourselves, otherwise take another turn.
  -->
  <state id="judging">
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ ONE PLACE, AND IT IS THE ENTRY RATHER THAN THE EDGES. Every way into
      this state is a `turn.done` — from `working` and from `awaiting_human` —
      so `_event.data` here is that event's on both paths, and the assignment
      runs exactly once per judged turn.

      Writing it on the `judge` transitions instead would be FOUR copies of one
      line, which is what this document warns against wherever it composes
      rather than repeats: the failure of letting copies drift is silent, and
      here it would be silent in the worst way — the guard that eventually reads
      this would see a level from some earlier turn and be right about a session
      that no longer exists.
    -->
    <onentry>
      <assign location="context" expr="_event.data.context"/>
    </onentry>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE RUN'S BUDGET IS SPENT ON A CEILING THIS DOCUMENT CANNOT SEE, so ask
      for the account and end — the SAME state `max_turns` reaches below, entered
      by the only door the outside has.

      `max_turns` is this document's own budget and the guard below reads it. The
      run is ALSO bounded by its host's guardrails — a step count, a spend, a wall
      clock — and none of those is expressible here: they count things the machine
      has no name for, and they are decided between steps rather than inside one.
      Before this edge a run stopped by one of them was simply never stepped
      again: measured, the loop was left standing in `working` or `judging` with
      its agent at rest, and the run answered `exhausted` and nothing at all —
      exactly the silence the guard below had just removed from the ending next
      door. **Three ways to run out, one of them explained.**

      ⚠⚠⚠ IT IS FIRST, ABOVE *the milestone was reached*, and that is the OPPOSITE
      order from `max_turns` below. The reason is who decided. `max_turns` is a
      number in this document, so an agent that reaches its milestone on the last
      turn has plainly done what it was asked and deserves the reflection. This
      one is a ceiling the run has ALREADY been stopped by, from outside, and
      nothing said in a courtesy turn can un-end it — sending such a run to
      `reflecting` would spend the window replacing a session for work that will
      never happen.

      ⚠⚠ IT DOES NOT COST A RUN THAT IS NOT STOPPING SHORT ANYTHING: the driver
      publishes `false` on every other judgement, and Lua's only false values are
      `nil` and `false`.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND WHEN IT IS NOT `false` IT IS THE CEILING'S OWN WORD, not merely
      `true`. Three of the run's ceilings come through this one door and they are
      three different facts about what to raise — a step count, a spend, a wall
      clock — so a boolean here left `stop_prompt` telling every one of them it
      had run out of TURNS (register item 264). The value is
      `Ceiling::wire_str`'s, a closed set of non-empty words, which is what keeps
      this guard a truth test: Lua's only false values are `nil` and `false`, so
      an EMPTY word would fire this edge on every judgement of every run. Nothing
      can publish one — there is no `Ceiling` that spells itself `''` — and a
      gate holds that rather than this comment.
    -->
    <transition event="judge" cond="_event.data.stop_short" target="stopping">
      <assign location="stop_reason" expr="_event.data.stop_short"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE MILESTONE IS REACHED, SO ASK WHAT THE NEXT ONE IS. This used to go
      straight to `closing`, and that made a run exactly as long as its first
      checkpoint: the owner's own debt-repayment loop paid ONE item, said the
      marker, and converged after a single working turn — judged straight into
      `closing` and out — against a request to keep going until every debt was
      repaid.

      A reflection is already the state that decides where the work goes next, so
      *this step is done* and *the budget came round* now arrive at the same
      place and differ only in what brought them. The run ends when the agent
      says the NORTH STAR is reached, which `reflecting` asks for and nothing
      else does.

      ⚠⚠ WHAT IT COSTS, STATED: reaching a milestone now spends a reflection turn
      before anything else happens, where it used to spend a closing one. A run
      whose LAST turn reaches its milestone therefore reflects, restarts, and
      then meets the budget guard below on the next judgement — one session
      replacement for nothing. Accepted rather than guarded around, because the
      alternative (testing the budget first) would report `exhausted` for a run
      that had plainly reached what it was asked for.
    -->
    <transition event="judge" cond="_event.data.done" target="reflecting">
      <assign location="reflect_reason" expr="'milestone'"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE BUDGET IS SPENT, SO ASK FOR AN ACCOUNT BEFORE ENDING. This used to
      go straight to `exhausted`, and that made the ONE ending a person most
      wants explained the one ending that explained nothing: a run that reached
      its north star handed back its agent's own report, and a run that stopped
      short handed back the single word `exhausted`.

      ⚠⚠ IT IS THE ONLY UNFINISHED ENDING THAT CAN BE ASKED, and the other three
      are refused by mechanism rather than by scope:

        cancelled  the run's context is gone — there is no time left to spend on
                   a turn, and the driver is about to interrupt the very pane the
                   question would go into.
        failed     every producer of `fail` is a session or a datamodel that has
                   stopped answering, so there is nothing left to ask WITH: the
                   brief was refused at the door, the prompts cannot be read out
                   of the datamodel, or the replacement session never came up.
        blocked    reached only through `awaiting_human`, and all three of ITS
                   doors say the same thing: somebody or something else owns
                   that pane. `screen.none` and `redirect.none` leave a dialog
                   nobody answered up, and typing a question into that pane
                   ANSWERS THE DIALOG — which is exactly what those two refuse
                   to do, and for the same reason. `turn.interrupted` and `hold`
                   put a PERSON at the keyboard, and a run must not prompt
                   underneath one. A question needs a pane this run may type in,
                   and by the time `blocked` is reached there is not one.

      So the run's account is asked for wherever it CAN be, and where it cannot
      the reason is a fact about the pane rather than a decision anybody took.

      ⚠⚠ AND THIS IS THE ONE CEILING NO DRIVER CAN SEE, so this edge is the only
      place `turns` can be written down — see `stop_reason`. The word is spelled
      here rather than left to the absence of the other door's: a reader that
      told the two apart by *which key is missing* is the reading register items
      264 and 265 are about.
    -->
    <transition event="judge" cond="turns &gt;= max_turns" target="stopping">
      <assign location="stop_reason" expr="'turns'"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ A STANDING INSTRUCTION HAS FIRED THAT THE PROMPTS DO NOT YET CARRY, so
      reflect NOW rather than at the next multiple of `reflect_every`.

      This is not a budget; it is a correctness edge. `screening` refuses a call
      and says what to do instead, and the very next `turn_prompt` asks for the
      original milestone again — measured on a six-turn run as ONE instruction
      against SIX re-issues, with a live agent reporting the deadlock in words.
      Adopting the instruction means composing it into the prompts, which happens
      in `priming`, which is reached only through a restart.

      ⚠ A SEPARATE TRANSITION from the budget one rather than an `or` in its
      guard, because they are different reasons and a reader of this document
      should meet them separately.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND A RUN'S TRACE NOW TELLS THEM APART, WHICH IT DID NOT. This comment
      used to end *"which one fired is not published anywhere, and saying so here
      is cheaper than a comment that implies it is"* — and it was true for as long
      as both edges existed. Every one of these three assignments was written and
      read by nothing but the livelock guard in `reflecting`, so three runs with
      three different remedies — *look at the checkpoint the agent chose*, *look
      at the instruction and the dialog behind it*, *nothing, this is the loop's
      own housekeeping* — left journals that were byte-for-byte identical on the
      line that mattered.

      The driver reads `reflect_reason` ON THE EDGE THAT ENTERS `reflecting` and
      puts it in that line. **So the assignments below are load-bearing**: an edge
      into this state that assigns nothing does not merely leave the walk silent,
      it reports the PREVIOUS reflection's cause, because nothing clears the
      variable on entry. A gate reads this file to hold that
      (`every_edge_into_reflecting_says_why_in_a_word_this_driver_knows`) and
      three runs hold what each word means
      (`the_walk_says_why_a_run_stopped_to_reflect`).
    -->
    <transition event="judge" cond="screened &gt; screened_carried" target="reflecting">
      <assign location="reflect_reason" expr="'instruction'"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="judge" cond="turns_since_reflect &gt;= reflect_every" target="reflecting">
      <assign location="reflect_reason" expr="'budget'"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="judge" target="working">
      <send event="prompt.turn"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    Improve the loop's own setup from the record so far: what the next milestone
    is, what the next session should read, and the standing instructions this run
    has been given. None of those takes effect until a session reads them, which
    is why this state's only forward exit is a restart.

    ⚠⚠⚠ IT IS A TURN, AND THE AGENT IS THE ONE ASKED. `reflect_prompt` goes into
    the pane on entry — the same way `priming` sends the start prompt and
    `closing` the end one — and the driver waits for that turn to end exactly as
    it waits for a working one. What the agent writes back is what `restarting`
    hands the replacement.

    Until that existed the only thing a reflection could carry across a session
    boundary was something the AUTHOR had written (a fired `screen_rule`'s text),
    so a run's milestone was fixed for its whole life by whoever started it. The
    shape of the fix is not that the driver became cleverer: it is that the party
    who has been doing the work is now asked about it, because *where the work
    should go next* is a judgement about the work and this driver cannot make one.

    ⚠⚠ THE TURN IS NOT JUDGED, which is why there is no edge to `judging`. A
    reflection is the loop asking about itself, not the agent making progress on
    the milestone, so it does not spend `max_turns` and cannot converge a run.

    ⚠⚠⚠ AND A DIALOG HERE WAKES SOMEBODY, where the same dialog during work is
    screened. `screening` returns to `working` unconditionally, and this document
    has no history state — so a reflection routed through it would resume the WORK
    loop having silently abandoned the reflection, and the run would carry on with
    the milestone nobody had updated. Waking a person is the honest answer until
    the document can resume a screened state; the prompt asks for no tool
    precisely to keep the case rare. ⚠ Registered as owed rather than hidden.
  -->
  <state id="reflecting">
    <onentry>
      <assign location="turns_since_reflect" expr="0"/>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠ BOTH REASONS TO BE HERE ARE CLEARED ON ENTRY, not on the way out. A
        reflection that finds nothing worth changing answers `reflect.none` and
        goes back to work, and if this only moved on `reflect.applied` that run
        would be sent straight back here by the same guard on the next
        judgement — for ever, having judged no further turn.
      -->
      <assign location="screened_carried" expr="screened"/>
      <!--
        ⚠ THE SEND `Owed` READS. `priming` and `closing` announce their prompts
        the same way, and the driver's table keys those two on the STATE rather
        than on the transition that reached it — which is what makes this one
        correct however a run arrives here (the correctness edge or the budget).
      -->
      <send event="prompt.reflect"/>
    </onentry>
    <!--
      The driver rewrote the loop's parts and/or its environment; adopt them.

      ⚠⚠⚠ THE PARTS, NOT THE PROMPTS, and the change is forced rather than
      tidy. This transition used to assign `start_prompt` and `turn_prompt`
      whole, from text the driver had composed — a second author for the one
      thing this template exists to own. Now that `priming` composes, a prompt
      assigned here would be overwritten on the way through it, which is a
      document that contradicts itself. Handing over the PARTS is the shape that
      survives: `restarting -> priming` recomposes from them, so a reflection
      changes what the loop is aiming at and the template still decides how that
      is said.

      ⚠ `north_star` is deliberately absent. It is where the loop is ultimately
      going, and a run that may rewrite its own destination cannot be said to
      have reached it.
    -->
    <transition event="reflect.applied" target="reviewing">
      <assign location="milestone" expr="_event.data.milestone"/>
      <assign location="reference" expr="_event.data.reference"/>
      <assign location="standing" expr="_event.data.standing"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      Nothing worth changing: carry on without paying for a restart.

      ⚠⚠⚠ IT STILL ADOPTS THE STANDING LIST, and that is what keeps this state
      from growing one. `screen.matched` APPENDS, so an agent that asks the same
      thing twice puts the same line in twice — and reflection is where the list
      is normalised, on BOTH ways out. Assigning it only on `reflect.applied`
      would leave duplicates behind on every reflection that changed nothing,
      and each duplicate is a `standing` the prompts do not carry, which is a
      restart. **A run whose agent kept asking the same question would replace
      its session every time.**
    -->
    <transition event="reflect.none" target="working">
      <assign location="standing" expr="_event.data.standing"/>
      <send event="prompt.turn"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE REFLECTION TURN'S OTHER TWO ENDINGS, and they go where a turn that
      nobody can carry on from has always gone.

      A reflection is a turn, so it can be blocked by a dialog or interrupted by
      a person exactly as a working turn can. Neither can be answered here: see
      this state's own comment for why `screening` is not reachable from a
      reflection, and a person typing in the pane is somebody the loop must not
      prompt underneath. `awaiting_human` is what this document already says
      happens when the run needs somebody, and both arrive there rather than
      inventing a new ending.

      ⚠ The reflection is LOST when either fires — the milestone stays what it
      was and `turns_since_reflect` has already been cleared, so the run carries
      on toward the old checkpoint and reflects again at the next budget point.
      That is the safe direction: the alternative is adopting a milestone from a
      turn that did not finish.
    -->
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE AGENT SAYS THERE IS NOTHING LEFT — the ONE way a run reaches its
      closing report, and the only place `north_star_marker` is heard.

      The reflection asks two questions in one prompt: *what is the next
      checkpoint* and *is the whole thing finished*. This is the second answer,
      and it is deliberately the ONLY route to `closing`: a run's account is
      written once, about the whole run, rather than once per milestone.

      ⚠ `north_star` is the one part a reflection may never rewrite (see
      `reflect.applied`), which is what makes this answer meaningful — the agent
      is reporting against a destination it could not have moved.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND *THERE IS NOTHING LEFT* IS TWO DIFFERENT SENTENCES. The driver
      raises this event from two places and they are not the same run: the agent
      said `north_star_marker`, or the milestone was reached and the reflection
      named no successor — the livelock this state exists to prevent. Both end
      `converged` and, until `done_reason` existed, both wrote one arrow. The
      word says which; the guard is why a raise that forgot to say cannot close
      a run in silence. See `done_reason`'s own comment for both.
    -->
    <transition event="reflect.done" cond="_event.data.done_reason" target="closing">
      <assign location="done_reason" expr="_event.data.done_reason"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="turn.blocked" target="awaiting_human"/>
    <transition event="turn.interrupted" target="awaiting_human"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    ⚠⚠⚠ LOOK AT WHAT THIS RUN'S OWN CLOSED SESSIONS DID, before opening another
    one that could do it again.

    # Why it sits HERE and nowhere else

    The things a review could improve about a session — what it starts knowing —
    are read when the session STARTS. So the only moment an answer can change
    anything is the gap between deciding to restart and restarting, and that gap
    is exactly one transition wide. Placed after `restarting` the finding would
    arrive at a session already briefed; placed before `reflecting` it would
    describe sessions the run had not finished with.

    ⚠⚠⚠ IT IS A SEPARATE STATE FROM `restarting` FOR THAT STATE'S OWN REASON,
    written one door down: *replacing a pane happens ONCE*, and a state that both
    reviewed and replaced would leave the driver remembering which half it had
    done. Arriving here means REVIEW; arriving at `restarting` means REPLACE.

    # ⚠⚠⚠ A REVIEW MUST NEVER BE ABLE TO STOP THE RUN

    Every ending leads to `restarting`. The review is ADVICE about work already
    finished, and the run was going to restart whether or not anybody looked — so
    a review that finds nothing, cannot read the records, or breaks outright must
    cost the run exactly one transition and no more. That is why there is no edge
    from here to `failed`: a `fail` raised by a reviewer is not a failure of the
    loop, and giving it that power would let a file nobody was waiting on end a
    run that was working.

    ⚠ `cancel` is the one exception, and it is not the review's doing: a person
    or a ceiling ending the run is answered here as it is answered everywhere.

    ⚠⚠ THE DRIVER OWNS THE REVIEW'S LIFETIME because this document cannot — see
    `crate::review::ContextReview`, which holds the measurement of why an
    `<invoke>` was not usable. Leaving this state must drop it, and that is a
    property a gate has to hold rather than the engine.
  -->
  <state id="reviewing">
    <onentry>
      <send event="review.begin"/>
    </onentry>
    <!--
      The review carried a line for the session that does not exist yet. ⚠ It is
      assigned rather than appended — see `carried`.
    -->
    <transition event="review.done" target="restarting">
      <assign location="carried" expr="_event.data.carried"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ NOTHING TO CARRY IS THE HEALTHY ANSWER, AND IT MUST CLEAR THE SLOT.
      A run whose last review found a habit and whose next one finds none has
      stopped doing it; leaving the old line in place would brief every later
      session about a habit that is over, and the loop would keep correcting
      something nobody was doing any more.
    -->
    <transition event="review.none" target="restarting">
      <assign location="carried" expr="''"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    Close the inner pane and open a fresh one. The new session reads whatever
    `reflecting` wrote on its way up.
  -->
  <state id="restarting">
    <onentry>
      <send event="session.replace"/>
    </onentry>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE REPLACEMENT AND THE WAIT ARE TWO STATES, and the reason is this
      document's own rule that the machine instructs its driver THROUGH ITS
      STATE.

      Replacing a pane happens ONCE; waiting for the agent inside the fresh one
      to come up takes as many looks as it takes. Written as one state, the
      driver would have to remember whether it had already done the replacing —
      and a driver that gets that wrong opens a pane per pump, which is a run
      that spawns agent processes without limit. Nothing about the document
      would have said it was wrong.

      So arriving here means REPLACE, and arriving at `resuming` means WAIT.
      Neither the driver nor a reader of the trace has to infer which half a run
      is in.
    -->
    <transition event="session.replaced" target="resuming"/>
    <!-- A session that will not come back is a failed run, not a stuck one. -->
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    A fresh inner session exists and its agent is starting. Nothing is typed
    until it is up: a prompt into a pane whose agent has existed for ten
    milliseconds is echoed by the terminal's own line discipline and read back
    as delivered, which is measured and is what `idle`'s barrier exists for.
    This is that same barrier, on the pane that replaced the old one.
  -->
  <state id="resuming">
    <transition event="session.ready" target="priming"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    ⚠⚠⚠ A DIALOG THAT WOULD COMMIT A DESIGN DECISION, TURNED DOWN AND REDIRECTED.

    The same act `screening` performs on a matched rule — refuse the call with
    the product's key, then say what to do instead — reached by a different
    route. It is a state of its own rather than an arm of `screening` because a
    reader of a finished run has to be able to tell WHICH authority acted: a
    quote the author wrote, or a judgement about this one dialog. `screening`
    carries the rule's own words out on `Screened::when`; this carries the
    criterion.

    ⚠ `redirect.none` is the honest exit and not a defensive one. The refusing
    key may not take the dialog off the screen — `not_dismissed` where it was
    watched for the whole bound, `unwitnessed` where the run ended inside that
    bound and nobody looked — and a dialog still up reads an Enter as an answer
    to itself. So nothing is typed and the person is woken, exactly as they are
    when no rule claims a question. ⚠ Which refusals arrive is `Unanswered`'s
    business, as it is on `screen.none`; naming them is not counting them.
  -->
  <state id="redirecting">
    <onentry>
      <send event="redirect.begin"/>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="redirect.done" target="working">
      <assign location="redirected" expr="redirected + 1"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="redirect.none" target="awaiting_human"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
  </state>

  <!-- The goal was met; ask for the closing report before ending. -->
  <state id="closing">
    <onentry>
      <send event="prompt.end"/>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="turn.done" target="converged"/>
    <transition event="turn.blocked" target="screening"/>
    <transition event="turn.interrupted" target="awaiting_human"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    ⚠⚠⚠ THE BUDGET IS SPENT; ASK WHERE THE RUN GOT TO, THEN END IT.

    `closing`'s twin for the ending that did NOT get there — see `stop_prompt`
    for why the question is a different one and not that one reused.

    ⚠⚠ TWO DOORS, ONE STATE, and they are two different budgets rather than two
    routes to one. `turns >= max_turns` is this document's own; `stop_short` is a
    ceiling of the RUN's that this machine cannot see at all (a step count, a
    spend, a wall clock). What they share is everything after the guard: the same
    question, the same reader, the same three endings. A second state would have
    been a second account to keep right.

    ⚠⚠⚠ BUT FOUR REASONS, NOT TWO — the outside door carries any of the run's
    three — AND THE QUESTION USED TO NAME THE WRONG ONE. `stop_prompt` was a
    single sentence opening *"This run has spent its whole turn budget"*, so
    three runs in four were told something false in the one turn where they are
    asked to account for themselves, and that sentence is not a journal line a
    reader can weigh: IT IS TYPED INTO THE AGENT'S PANE. So both doors now write
    `stop_reason`, and the entry below composes the ceiling's own clause into the
    question. Register item 264, and 265 for the walk that reads the same word.

    ⚠⚠⚠ EVERY WAY THIS TURN CAN END ARRIVES AT `exhausted`, AND THAT IS THE
    WHOLE SHAPE OF THE STATE. The ending was decided by the guard that got here:
    the run is out of turns, and nothing the agent does with this last question
    can change that. An account is a courtesy on top of a verdict already
    reached, so **a run that cannot be given one is still that run** — a peer
    that stops to ask, and a person who takes the pane, both end `exhausted`
    with no account rather than being routed somewhere that would treat this
    turn as work.

    ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE ALTERNATIVE IS NOT MERELY UNTIDY, IT IS UNBOUNDED. Both guards
    that reach here stay true for the rest of the run — `turns >= max_turns` is
    arithmetic that cannot go back down, and `stop_short` is a latch over a
    ceiling that has already bound — so any edge from here back into the working
    cycle would come round to `judging`, take the same transition, and ask for
    another account, for ever. `screening` and `awaiting_human` are both such
    edges, and they are what `closing` uses. This state must not have them, and
    the reason is arithmetic rather than taste.

    ⚠⚠ WHAT IT COSTS, STATED. A run out of turns now spends one more turn before
    it ends, and that turn is bounded by the run's own guardrails rather than by
    `max_turns` — which is already spent. So a peer that hangs on this last
    question turns an `exhausted — turns` into an `exhausted — duration`: the
    same ending, reported against a different ceiling. Accepted, because the
    alternative is refusing to ask the question at all; the run's journal carries
    the walk either way.

    ⚠ `cancel` and `fail` keep their ordinary targets, unlike the turn's own
    endings above. Those two are facts about the RUN and not about this turn: a
    person cancelling here has stopped the run, and reporting `exhausted` for it
    would tell them their own act was a budget running out.
  -->
  <state id="stopping">
    <onentry>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠ THE CEILING GOES INTO THE QUESTION, and this is the whole of register
        item 264. Both doors above have just written `stop_reason`; this is where
        the word becomes a sentence the agent can act on. Composed on ENTRY, for
        `priming`'s reason one state over: the parts cannot have changed since,
        and the answer does not exist until the edge that got here was taken.

        ⚠⚠ AN UNKNOWN REASON PUTS THE WORD `nil` IN THE SENTENCE — measured, not
        assumed: the concatenation succeeds and the agent reads it. `stop_said`
        is held TOTAL over the driver's ceilings by a gate that reads this file,
        which is what makes that unreachable; see its own comment.
      -->
      <assign location="stop_prompt"
              expr="stop_opening + ' ' + stop_said[stop_reason] + ' ' + stop_asking"/>
      <send event="prompt.stop"/>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="turn.done" target="exhausted"/>
    <transition event="turn.blocked" target="exhausted"/>
    <transition event="turn.interrupted" target="exhausted"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
  </state>

  <final id="converged"/>
  <final id="exhausted"/>
  <final id="failed"/>
  <final id="cancelled"/>
  <final id="blocked"/>
</scxml>
